From 6c5213a5291972b20ece313ddd006ac677071849 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 12:35:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Scope analysis, history, and investigations to the
 logged-in user

Previously user_id was a client-supplied value trusted as-is on
/analyze and /history, and /investigation/<id> had no ownership
check at all -- anyone could read anyone else's data. Google OAuth
set a session but nothing downstream used it.

- Add login_required decorator deriving user_id from session["user"]
  (set at /auth/google/callback), the only trusted source now.
- /analyze, /history, /investigation/<id> all require login and are
  scoped to the session user; /investigation/<id> 404s (not 403) on
  a valid id owned by someone else.
- google_callback now registers the user row at login time and
  redirects to FRONTEND_URL (env var) instead of a hardcoded
  localhost; added /auth/me and /auth/logout.
- CORS switched from origins="*" (incompatible with cookies) to the
  explicit FRONTEND_URL with supports_credentials=True.
- Frontend: api.js sends credentials:"include" and drops user_id
  from every call; app.jsx loads the real session user, gates
  Analyze/History behind login, and the topbar shows the actual
  signed-in user with working logout. LoginPage uses VITE_API_URL
  instead of a hardcoded backend URL.
